  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe weather risk will continue through this evening with\nthe greatest risk across central VA, southern MD, and the Appalachian\nregion. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\n18Z RAOB plots along with WV imagery show an unusually deep\ntrough across the Great Lks into the OH Valley with an 85-kt 250\nmb jet streak measured at Davenport, IA and Wilmington, OH and\n55-kt 500 mb wind speeds measured at ILN and IAD. 18Z NUCAPS\ndata showed the greatest instability and moisture axis resides\nalong the AKQ and LWX borders. This looks like it will be\nbattleground zone for the most severe wx and flash flood risk\nthrough this evening into the early morning hours. Multiple \nrounds of thunderstorms are expected, particularly across the \nsouth where training convection is most likely. Convection \nacross OH and southwest PA will have less instability to work \nwith, but stronger shear which may still pose a severe wind \nrisk. Convection will gradually sink through the evening\ntapering from northwest to southeast, but convection will likely\nlinger along the AKQ and LWX borders well through the night. \n\nStill a risk of severe wx Wed, but likely for areas south of the\nCapital Beltway and southern MD. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures and mainly dry conditions \nare expected Thursday through the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA sprawling area of high pressure will build to our north for \nThursday and Friday, before shifting off the New England Coastline \non Saturday. Northerly flow ahead of the approaching high will \nadvect a much cooler and drier airmass into the region for Thursday. \nWell below normal temperatures and dewpoints are forecast for late \nJuly, with high temperatures only in the upper 70s/low 80s. Dewpoints \nin the 50s will also make it feel much less humid than is typical \nfor July. Mostly sunny skies will accompany the well below normal \ntemperatures/dewpoints. Temperatures will stay well below normal for \nFriday and Saturday within easterly flow. Both temperatures and \ndewpoints begin to rise again on Sunday as flow turns southerly, \nwith highs in the mid to upper 80s and dewpoints in the 60s. The \nreturn of higher dewpoint air should lead to slightly greater \nchances for afternoon/evening showers and thunderstorms, but most \nlocations will likely still stay dry.\n\nForecast confidence decreases substantially by Monday and Tuesday, \nbut chances for showers and thunderstorms may further increase as \nmultiple shortwave disturbances move through in northwesterly flow \naloft. Depending on how the forecast details evolve, there could be \na conditional threat for severe thunderstorms on either Monday or \nTuesday.",
